★ [saf-beliefs-016] (confidence: 0.72)
  "Risk Compounds Nonlinearly as AI Systems Interact" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that describes how the interaction of multiple AI systems leads to risks that multiply rather than simply adding up. 
Encompasses: Scenarios where a minor error in one AI can trigger a cascading failure across interconnected systems, making overall system behavior unpredictable.
  Vulnerability: Complex systems engineering has decades of successful experience managing nonlinear failure risks through redundancy, fault isolation, circuit-breaker patterns, and rigorous simulation — and there is no demonstrated empirical evidence yet that AI multi-agent systems specifically produce catastrophic cascades at rates meaningfully higher than well-engineered non-AI distributed systems, making the "multiplication" framing more theoretical than proven.
★ [saf-beliefs-099] (confidence: 0.62)
  "AI Monocultures and Multi-Agent Interactions Amplify Systemic Fragility" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that describes how the interaction of multiple AI systems, or reliance on monolithic architectures, leads to unpredictable, cascading failures and the propagation of unsafe practices, multiplying risks beyond simple aggregation.
Encompasses: Emergent errors in multi-agent systems, systemic fragility from monoculture, and the spread of dangerous behaviors.
  Vulnerability: Empirical evidence of large-scale AI monoculture failures or dangerous multi-agent cascades remains limited; existing multi-agent deployments (e.g., algorithmic trading, cloud orchestration) have largely been managed through engineering discipline and circuit-breaker mechanisms, suggesting that systemic fragility may be addressable through conventional systems-engineering practices without invoking novel AI-specific risk frameworks.
★ [saf-beliefs-044] (confidence: 0.55)
  "AI Companies Reduce Costs by Locating Compute in Pollution Havens" — A Belief within safetyist discourse that AI datacenters are strategically located in regions with weak regulations or cheap, carbon-intensive energy to reduce costs. 
Encompasses: Environmental arbitrage practices.
  Vulnerability: Many AI datacenter location decisions are driven primarily by proximity to renewable energy sources, reliable grid infrastructure, cooling resources, network latency, and talent pools rather than regulatory laxity; moreover, major hyperscalers have made substantial long-term power purchase agreements for renewable energy, suggesting the "pollution haven" framing overstates deliberate environmental arbitrage and conflates general cost-optimization with intentional regulatory evasion.

